<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Loango</h1><p class="st-meta">1550 CE – 1883 CE (approx.) · capital Mbanza Loango</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Vili kingdom of the Loango coast, a major Atlantic trading power known for its raffia cloth, copper and ivory and later for slaves.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Loango emerged among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vili-people">Vili</a> north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-river">Congo River</a>, probably by the 15th century, and by the mid-16th century had shaken off any dependence on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kingdom of Kongo</a>. Its king, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maloango">Maloango</a>, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbanza-loango">Mbanza Loango</a> (Buali) near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loango-bay">Loango Bay</a>, the kingdom's main harbour.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maloango">Maloango</a> was a sacred ruler chosen from princes of the royal matrilineage, assisted by ministers responsible for trade, war and the coast. Loango exported fine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raffia-cloth">raffia cloth</a>, used as currency in the region, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> from the Mindouli mines, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and dyewood. From the 17th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, English and French ships bought slaves at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loango-bay">Loango Bay</a> in large numbers.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> and long interregnums left the crown weak in the 19th century. In 1883 the coast came under French protection, and the area became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-congo">French Congo</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maloango">Maloango</a> title survives as a traditional office.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-loango.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Loango" width="320" height="211"><figcaption>Image: Dekodrak · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Kingdom_of_Loango.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1550 CE – 1883 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mbanza Loango</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional Kongo religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kikongo (Vili)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/republic-of-the-congo">Republic of the Congo</a>, <a href="/countries/angola">Angola</a>, <a href="/countries/gabon">Gabon</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Loango, Lwangu</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1716&amp;focus=kingdom-of-loango" aria-label="Kingdom of Loango on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5008 2354 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Loango lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5328 2610L5395 2668M5328 2610L5262 2418"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5395 2668h0M5262 2418h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5328 2610h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5328 2610h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1716&amp;focus=kingdom-of-loango">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
